   "answer": "## Result: HITI-2c gives more mCherry-positive cells than HDR\n\nThe Welch t test gives p = 0.00629 for the difference in percent of mCherry-positive cells. HITI-2c is higher. The cutoff is 4252.7 on the raw PE-CF594-A scale, set from control_rep1.\n\n### Steps\n1. Cutoff: I measured PE-CF594-A in control_rep1 with population_stats. Settings: all events, no transform, no compensation. The control_rep1 cutoff is 4252.7. The tool did not report the percentile that sets it. Control_rep1 has 0.10% of events above the cutoff.\n2. Parent gate: all events. No scatter gate was set. Parent counts are 14,657 to 17,504 events per sample.\n3. Counts: I counted the events above 4252.7 in the six design files with population_stats (flowCore 4.6.1).\n4. Design file: I wrote a design file with the group HDR (bv_hdr_rep1 to rep3) and HITI-2c (bv_hiti2c_rep1 to rep3).\n5. Test: compare_groups ran a Welch t test on the percent of parent, with no correction, because there is one comparison.\n\n### Per sample\n\n| Sample | Parent events | mCherry+ events | % of parent | MFI of positive events |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HDR rep1 | 15,688 | 663 | 4.23 | 24,271 |\n| HDR rep2 | 17,504 | 777 | 4.44 | 23,573 |\n| HDR rep3 | 17,158 | 718 | 4.18 | 23,646 |\n| HITI-2c rep1 | 16,334 | 3,180 | 19.47 | 27,026 |\n| HITI-2c rep2 | 15,815 | 3,633 | 22.97 | 28,740 |\n| HITI-2c rep3 | 14,657 | 2,751 | 18.77 | 30,119 |\n\nUntransfected controls: 0.10%, 0.02% and 0.08% of events are above the cutoff (13, 2 and 9 events).\n\n### Group comparison\n- HDR: mean 4.28%, SD 0.14, n = 3.\n- HITI-2c: mean 20.40%, SD 2.25, n = 3.\n- Difference (HITI-2c minus HDR): 16.12 percentage points.\n- Welch t test: p = 0.00629.\n\n### Sensitivity to the cutoff\nEach control replicate gives a different cutoff:\n- control_rep2 (cutoff 2699.4): HDR 4.98% (SD 0.22), HITI-2c 22.66% (SD 2.52), p = 0.00637.\n- control_rep3 (cutoff 3762.9): HDR 4.43% (SD 0.15), HITI-2c 20.95% (SD 2.27), p = 0.00606.\n\nThe direction and the p value stay the same with all three cutoffs.\n\n### Uncertain\n- The cutoff comes from one control file. I asked whether to pool the three controls. You chose to keep the record value.\n- n = 3 means three files. I do not know if each file is an independent transfection. If the tubes come from one batch, the p value overstates the evidence.\n- Percents depend on the cutoff. Compare percents only between samples that use the same cutoff.\n\n### Waiting for you\n- Confirm that each file is an independent replicate. This decides if the p value is valid.\n- Decide if the cutoff should use all three controls. This would change the percents.\n\nStep results that the answer does not mention, from the record:\n- n3 population_stats: 6 samples, 1 populations. Parent gate: all events. Transform: none. Compensation: none. Cutoffs: PE-CF594-A 2699.4.\n- n4 population_stats: 6 samples, 1 populations. Parent gate: all events. Transform: none. Compensation: none. Cutoffs: PE-CF594-A 3762.9.\n- n5 run_script: The script ran in /Volumes/T7/guided-analysis/tools/bench-runs/2026-10-09/claude_claude-haiku-5-5/aulicino2022-baculovirus-hdr/run1/home/sessions/20261009-040024-8040/work and wrote 1 new file(s) to /Volumes/T7/guided-analysis/tools/bench-runs/2026-10-09/claude_claude-haiku-5-5/aulicino2022-baculovirus-hdr/run1/home/sessions/20261009-040024-8040/work.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Decades of a log-amplified scale: 0 · Parent gate on scatter: none · Compensation of the samples: none · Transform of the fluorescence channels: none · Cofactor of the asinh transform: 150 · Cutoff between negative and positive: none · Percentile of the control for the cutoff: 99.9 · Statistical test for the groups: welch · Correction for many comparisons: none.",
